WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.100
Logs become more valuable when they are connected to monitoring and alerting.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:24.840
Alertable events should be chosen because they indicate meaningful risk or operational impact: repeated authentication failures, suspicious account recovery activity, privilege changes, access denials on sensitive resources, unusual administrative actions, dependency failures in critical workflows, or spikes in validation failures.

3
00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.020
The point is not to alert on everything.

4
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.320
The point is to surface events that deserve timely attention.

5
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.840
False positives and alert fatigue reduce response quality.

6
00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:40.140
If a team receives too many low-value alerts, important signals become easier to miss.

7
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:50.240
Detection use cases should define what behavior is being identified, why it matters, what fields are needed, what threshold or pattern is meaningful, and what responder action is expected.

8
00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:55.940
Dashboards can help show trends and health, but they should not replace clear alert logic and ownership.

9
00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:03.820
During incidents, logs help reconstruct activity, timelines, affected resources, decisions, and control behavior.

10
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:14.320
A responder may need to know when an issue started, which users were affected, which administrative actions occurred, which dependencies failed, and whether security controls fired.

11
00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:18.100
Preserving relevant logs is part of incident handling.

12
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:23.720
After the incident, teams should improve logging and alerting based on what was hard to answer.

13
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:28.300
Every incident is a chance to make future evidence cleaner and faster to use.
